{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Montrealer, a five-railroad joint Washington-Montreal overnight train and the direct historical predecessor of Amtrak's Vermonter. Route geometry is taken from the modern Amtrak route's own GTFS-sourced alignment for this corridor (see amtrak.geojson), since the historical train ran the same physical line; where the historical train ran beyond or differently from today's Amtrak route, that is described in the segment notes. Era boundaries follow real, sourced milestones; headway_min and avg_speed_kmh within each era are estimates, not from real timetables.", "source": "Wikipedia (Montrealer, Vermonter), american-rails.com", "pulled": "20260817", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "PRR / New Haven / B&M / Central Vermont / Canadian National", "line": "New Haven: Montrealer", "id": "montrealer", "color": "#7b4397", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 65, "speed_source": "estimated/blended across the line's history; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1924, "end": 1966, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "The Montrealer was inaugurated in 1924 as a joint overnight Washington-Montreal service of five railroads: the Pennsylvania, the New Haven, the Boston & Maine, the Central Vermont and the Canadian National. Discontinued 1966. (This geometry follows Amtrak's Vermonter as far as St. Albans, VT; the Montrealer continued on to Montreal.) estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Montrealer, Vermonter), american-rails.com"]}, {"start": 1972, "end": 1995, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Amtrak revived the Montrealer Sept 30 1972 - the first train for which Amtrak hired its own onboard staff rather than contracting with the host railroad - running until 1995 apart from a two-year gap in the 1980s. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Montrealer, Vermonter), american-rails.com"]}, {"start": 1995, "end": 2025, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Replaced Apr 1 1995 by the daytime Vermonter, terminating at St. Albans instead of Montreal - see amtrak.geojson. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Montrealer, Vermonter), american-rails.com"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-74.77521, 40.20194], "to": [-74.53208, 40.38856], "chord_mi": 18.94, "traced_mi": 18.2, "snap_m": [8, 22], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Northeast Corridor (18.2 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}], "geometry_quality": "Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 1 ruled segment totalling 19 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed)."}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-77.0052, 38.89682], [-77.00067, 38.9103], [-76.98066, 38.91758], [-76.92288, 38.91618], [-76.90696, 38.92057], [-76.85391, 38.96739], [-76.77264, 39.01144], [-76.73992, 39.04514], [-76.72801, 39.0668], [-76.70766, 39.08455], [-76.69597, 39.12237], [-76.69724, 39.16219], [-76.69239, 39.18566], [-76.69733, 39.2017